+*   **Sample Points for Evaluation:** 10,000 points uniformly sampled per mesh [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Image Feature Extractor:** ResNet-18 [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Omission of Regularization:** Self-intersecting triangles and tetrahedra occur when regularization terms are omitted [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Insufficient Regularization:** Using only the surface term yields good surface approximation but results in flipped tetrahedra and highly distorted faces [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Thin Structures:** Without the orientation term, thin structures (e.g., chair legs) may vanish, reducing physical feasibility [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Distorted Faces:** Without the robust projection term, the number of distorted triangle faces remains high [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Volumetric Mesh Optimization Objective](10955-volumetric-mesh-optimization-objective.md)
+The Volumetric Mesh Optimization Objective is a composite loss function used to regularize the geometry of tetrahedral meshes generated by neural networks, specifically within the Neural Volumetric Mesh Generator (NVMG) framework. It addresses the problem of generating high-quality, non-self-intersecting volumetric meshes from voxel-based representations by penalizing geometric distortions, orientation flips, and surface irregularities. It is distinguished from sibling methods by its explicit inclusion of an orientation preservation term based on the determinant of the tetrahedron transformation matrix and a Laplacian term that differentiates between volume and surface neighborhoods.
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Evaluation involves iterating over all tetrahedra and triangles, making it linear with respect to the number of elements $O(N_{tets} + N_{tris})$. Chamfer distance computation is $O(N_{sampled} \cdot N_{gt})$ without acceleration structures, or $O(N \log N)$ with spatial indexing [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the mesh size for storing vertex and connectivity data.
-*   **Measured Performance:** On the ShapeNetRender dataset (chair, bench, table categories), NVMG achieves Chamfer distances of 0.471 (chair), 0.342 (table), and 0.439 (bench) [S1, unofficial]. This outperforms baselines: Pixel2Mesh (0.610, 0.498, 0.624), GEOMetrics (0.823, 0.797, 0.690), and DeepMesh (0.514, 0.404, 0.516) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Preserves thin geometric features better than marching cube-based voxel-to-mesh methods [S1, unofficial]. Reduces flipped tets and distorted faces through specific regularization terms.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of regularization weights. The aspect ratio threshold (2.6) is specific to the evaluation context [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Single-view shape reconstruction, image-to-mesh generation, and user-driven shape editing where geometric validity is critical [S1, unofficial].
